From 2196349fc4ab9e63983b0b137114b101e2fb2363 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Cipoletti <adam@amcreativecoach.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 22:28:30 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 54/57] WebCore: update layer positions only where something
 moved

After layout, RenderLayer::updateLayerPositions walked every layer in the
page: recomputing each layer's position, its clip rects and its repaint
rects, and pushing and popping the geometry map along the way. On a page
with thousands of positioned elements that walk cost more than the layout
that prompted it, and almost all of it recomputed values that hadn't
changed.

Each renderer now carries the number of the layer position update its
subtree last needed (four bits in RenderElement, which had room to spare).
A renderer that was laid out marks itself and its ancestors with the view's
current generation, as do a layer that scrolled, one whose visible content
changed, and one that needs a full repaint; the view advances the
generation after each update. updateLayerPositions then skips a child whose
subtree carries an older generation, unless something above it moved, in
which case the subtree's positions really do have to be recomputed.

On the PowerBook G4, Speedometer 3.1:

  TodoMVC-Svelte-Complex-DOM         3393 -> 1255 ms
  TodoMVC-Preact-Complex-DOM         3462 -> 1396 ms
  TodoMVC-Angular-Complex-DOM        6498 -> 4319 ms
  TodoMVC-JavaScript-ES5             5456 -> 5308 ms

Checked against stale painting with a page that reaches its final state by
mutating the DOM, compared to the same page built in one go: the window's
own contents (CGWindowListCreateImage, not a fresh render) are identical
pixel for pixel, at the top of the page and scrolled.
